## What each new file pins (traceability to the plan's stated risk)

The plan's own risk table names the sharpest hazard: "a test that only checks
'no line exceeds 75 octets' would pass on output that folded mid-character
and corrupted the text." `test/export-ics.test.ts`'s folding test avoids that
trap directly:

1. A deterministically-constructed pt-BR title ("...suspensão...") whose
   75th content-line octet is proven (self-checked against the fixture, via
   `TextEncoder`, before trusting the rest of the assertion) to fall on a
   UTF-8 continuation byte — i.e. INSIDE the 2-byte "ã", not on a boundary.
2. Asserts no physical line exceeds 75 octets (necessary, not sufficient).
3. Asserts folding actually occurred (continuation lines exist) — otherwise
   the 75-octet check alone passes vacuously on a builder that never folds.
4. Asserts continuation lines begin with exactly one space.
5. Asserts the ROUND TRIP: reconstructing the logical SUMMARY line from its
   physical parts (stripping CRLF + the one leading space per continuation)
   reproduces the original title byte-for-byte, accents intact — the
   assertion a mid-character split cannot pass.

