## Addendum (2026-09-04): closed a review-flagged gap, not a rubric citation

test-reviewer's pass on the original two files found no rubric violation but
flagged, correctly, that no fixture exercised RFC 5545 §3.3.11 TEXT escaping
(`,`, `;`, `\`) even though the plan's own risk table named it alongside line
folding as part of the same content-line rules AC-7 invokes. Neither AC-7 nor
AC-8's PRD prose names escaping explicitly, so this maps to no rubric id —
it is a scope gap closed proactively, not a CHANGES_REQUESTED response.

Added ONE new test to the existing `describe("buildTasksIcs — AC-7 minimal
RFC 5545 validity", ...)` block in `test/export-ics.test.ts` (escaping is the
same RFC content-line discipline that block's other two tests already cover,
alongside CRLF and folding):

- **"escapes RFC 5545 TEXT-reserved characters in SUMMARY — comma, semicolon
  and backslash — and round-trips back to the original title"** — a pt-BR
  title (`"Comprar pão, leite e café; salvar em C:\backup"`) carrying one
  comma, one semicolon and one backslash. Asserts each is emitted
  backslash-escaped in SUMMARY; asserts the SUMMARY content line does NOT
  fold (explicit assertion, not just a sized-to-avoid-folding comment, per
  the instruction that escaping and folding must not silently interact in
  one fixture); and asserts a round trip — unescaping the emitted value
  (via a test-local `unescapeIcsText` oracle, never imported from the SUT)
  reproduces the original title exactly, the same fidelity standard the
  existing folding test already holds itself to.
- No newline character was added to the fixture — RFC 5545 also requires
  `\n`-escaping of embedded newlines, but the task's own instruction made
  that conditional ("if you include a newline"), and keeping the fixture to
  exactly the three characters test-reviewer named keeps the failure surface
  legible if this test ever goes red for the wrong reason.

